one of the key things that's happening  in the world right now is there's a  renewed interest in energy independence  given the russia ukraine conflict and  the fact that so much oil and natural  gas comes out of that region there's a  lot of people scratching their heads  saying hey shouldn't we actually be  dependent on ourselves for all of our  energy production having to rely on  countries like russia probably not a  good idea and so naturally that leads to  a conversation around nuclear power  there's all sorts of different  approaches that have been taken globally  places like germany who literally have  been shutting down nuclear power plants  and going ahead and actually increasing  their dependence on russian oil hasn't  turned out so well for them the united  states we haven't really been so  pro-nuclear also we haven't necessarily  been shutting down tons and tons of  plants but we definitely haven't been  building new ones so ultimately this  leads to a decision are we going to be  pro-nuclear as a country or are we not  if you're serious about climate change  if you're serious about energy  independence i think you naturally end  up being pro-nuclear and so the current  presidential administration president  biden and his team they're launching a  brand new 6 billion effort to save  distressed nuclear power plants now i  don't necessarily always agree with what  the president does but you got to give  him kudos for this he's a very very  intelligent decision to continue to make  investments in nuclear power within the  united states now let's take a look at  the nuclear power situation though there  are 55 commercial nuclear power plants  93 nuclear reactors across 28 u.s states  nuclear power already provides about 20  percent of electricity in the u.s and  about half the nation's carbon free  energy but most of them were built  between 1970 and 1990 and it's costing  more and more to operate them as they  get older and older infrastructure so  then if we take a look that the only  nuclear power plant in the united states  under construction is in georgia but  there's a dozen u.s commercial nuclear  power reactors that have closed in the  past decade before the license has  expired and that's largely due to  competition from cheaper sources of  energy massive operating losses or the  cost of major repairs and so we have to  decide if we're gonna be pro nuclear how  the hell can we make the economics work  well president biden and his  administration are stepping in with six  billion dollars to help prevent the  shutdown of future nuclear power plants  and to offset some of those major  operating losses if we then go and we  take a look the energy secretary making  the point that u.s nuclear power plants  contribute more than half of our carbon  free electricity president biden is  committed to keeping these plants active  to reach our clean energy goals we're  using every tool available to get this  country powered by clean energy by 2035  and that includes prioritizing our  existing nuclear fleet to allow for  continued emissions free electricity  generation and economic stability for  the communities leading this important  work now ultimately it is not a  coincidence that all of a sudden nuclear  has gotten back in vogue people are  really pro-nuclear because they're  seeing what's happening in russia  ukraine germany and elsewhere the united  states must become energy independent we  have the ability to do it but we've got  to embrace nuclear power and we have to  start to make the investments today this  stuff doesn't happen overnight it takes  time the united states can be the leader  of nuclear power we just have to have  the political will and the appetite to  do the hard work to make it happen hey  you did you like this video great we  make five of them a day and post them  here on this channel make sure you  subscribe like the video and see you  next time
 

